## Current Synthesis

### Idea

Make the hosted dashboard Work tab answer one primary operator question immediately: **what work
remains, in what recommended order, and where does each item belong?** Its default presentation is
an indented, expandable folder-style hierarchy derived from Tool Shed's established ownership and
parent/child relationships, not a newest-first ledger or node-and-arrow diagram.

Every dashboard-visible governed work item has exactly one canonical tree placement: it is either a
root or a child of one owning parent. Additional relationships remain secondary links and never
duplicate a subtree. Missing or ambiguous placement is surfaced in a bounded **Needs placement**
group rather than guessed. New governed work enters this hierarchy as soon as its authoritative
relationship is reported.

The default `Remaining` filter includes every item with an unfinished obligation: active, queued,
working, waiting, blocked, open or unreconciled outcome, closure debt, or completed work awaiting
Work5. It excludes an item only when it is terminal, recursively closed, reconciled, and carries no
remaining release obligation. Root chains are ordered by the locally reported planning order;
children preserve hierarchy and use dependency, milestone, Campaign queue, and stable fallback order.
Filtering never produces misleading orphans: matching descendants retain their ancestors as context.

Keep the existing flat list as an explicit audit/history view. Each tree row retains the useful
Document, Closure, Release, Outcome, and Reconciliation state. The command menu remains valuable but
secondary to this at-a-glance work-navigation outcome.

Give every tree entry a small square command button. Clicking the button opens a compact,
state-aware menu whose entries use clear human descriptions such as **Continue this campaign** or
**Investigate the blocker**. Selecting an entry copies the exact corresponding `ts:` command to the
browser clipboard for the operator to paste into Codex. It does not execute the command, submit an
action to the hosted service, or mutate local Tool Shed state.

### Why It Matters

The Overview summarizes one current node per chain, so it hides much of the remaining branch. The
current Work table shows the complete ledger newest-first, so completed history overwhelms current
work and the operator must reconstruct lineage from a Relationships column. Neither provides a
quick, ordered presentation of the complete remaining workload with its hierarchy intact.

A remaining-work tree makes ownership, branching, waiting siblings, release obligations, and the
next executable item visible together. The command menu then turns each row into a safe starting
point for local Codex work without requiring the operator to remember syntax or granting the hosted
dashboard control over the local workspace.

This extends two completed directions without reopening them: `IDEA-0012` established the bounded
relationship-aware hosted Work inventory, and `IDEA-0022` established descriptive clipboard-copy
commands as a safe bridge from advisory hosted state to locally authoritative Tool Shed actions.

### Desired Outcome

The Work tab opens on `Remaining` plus `Tree`. It presents the latest reporting instance's complete
unfinished workload as one or more expandable folder-style trees in recommended order. A concise
header reports remaining item and root-chain counts. Indentation and chevrons expose ownership;
one visible **Next** marker identifies the first executable item without hiding blocked or waiting
siblings. Existing state columns remain available for row-level inspection.

The tree is a projection, not a new authority. Root order follows the verified local planning
projection, then actionable lifecycle/readiness and stable ID. Within a root, semantic parent/child
placement always wins over global sorting; siblings use explicit dependency or milestone/Campaign
queue order when available and stable type/ID fallback otherwise. Completed ancestors required to
explain an unfinished descendant remain visible as subdued context. Pagination, when needed, keeps
whole roots together.

The filter offers `Remaining` by default, narrower operational states such as `Working`, `Blocked`,
and `Awaiting Work5`, plus explicit `All` history. A `Tree`/`List` view control keeps the flat ledger
available for global sorting and audit. Search and type filters preserve matching ancestors in Tree
view and clearly distinguish matching rows from context rows.

Every displayed Idea, Map, PRM, and Campaign has a compact square command-menu button. Its menu is
generated from a controlled local allowlist using only the artifact type, stable visible ID,
lifecycle, current blocker or finding state, and applicable release posture. Menu labels and short
descriptions explain the effect in plain language; the underlying exact command may remain
secondary or hidden. Selecting an item copies the command, gives accessible confirmation, and
never executes it.

Menus include common continuation and inspection routes appropriate to the artifact. Blocked work
adds commands that inspect or mitigate the blocker and, where valid, unblock the Campaign. A current
actionable loop finding may offer its exact `ts: resolve loop <LOOP-id>` route. Commands use stable
public IDs and force Codex to re-read authoritative local state after paste; they never contain
credentials, local paths, state tokens, raw diagnostic text, or pre-authorized mutations.

### Candidate Interaction

```text
Work                                      7 remaining items · 2 root chains
[Remaining ▾]  [Tree | List]  [All types ▾]

1  ▾ IDEA      Dashboard hierarchy and artifact commands
     ▾ MAP      Remaining-work dashboard direction
        ▾ PRM   Deliver the ordered hierarchy
           ● CAMP   Build remaining-work tree                         NEXT  [>_]
           ○ CAMP   Qualify and release                               Waiting [>_]

Needs placement
   ! Governed work with no valid canonical parent/root classification
```

Selecting `[>_]` on `CAMP-0166` could show descriptive choices such as:

- **Continue this campaign** — resume the exact Campaign from its current step.
- **Review current status** — explain progress, blockers, evidence, and the next safe action.
- **Investigate the blocker** — diagnose what prevents progress and recommend the smallest mitigation.
- **Return it to the queue** — clear a valid blocked state without starting execution.

Clicking a choice copies only its exact allowlisted `ts:` command and briefly confirms which action
was copied.

## Constraints And Non-Goals

- Local Tool Shed state remains authoritative; the hosted dashboard remains an advisory projection.
- The interface is a folder-style indented tree/table, not a graphical node-and-edge diagram.
- Preserve existing useful state dimensions rather than hiding them in the tree.
- Use reported stable relationships; do not infer hierarchy from titles, timestamps, or ID prefixes.
- Every displayed governed item has one canonical owning parent or is explicitly a root. Do not
  duplicate multi-linked work under several parents.
- Missing, cyclic, or ambiguous placement is visible and actionable; it never silently falls back to
  a plausible-looking hierarchy.
- Preserve hierarchy under filtering, searching, and pagination. Context ancestors must not inflate
  the reported matching-work count.
- Recommended order is locally derived and reported; the hosted dashboard cannot reorder work.
- Preserve reporting-instance boundaries and freshness; do not merge divergent instance trees.
- Command menus are built from a product-defined allowlist, controlled state, and stable public IDs.
- A click copies text to the clipboard only. It does not execute, enqueue, approve, or remotely
  deliver an action.
- Codex must resolve current project identity, state, authority, and safety after the command is pasted.
- Never copy credentials, filesystem paths, prompts, source text, transient tokens, raw diagnostics,
  shell commands, or unrestricted operator-authored text.
- History-only or resolved entries receive no mutation command. Read-only inspection may remain.
- Explicit non-goal: add a hosted-to-local action-intent channel, polling agent, or remote-control API.
- Explicit non-goal: duplicate lifecycle, blocker, reconciliation, PRM, Campaign, or release logic in
  the dashboard.

## Possibilities And Tradeoffs

| Possibility | Benefits | Costs / Risks | Evidence / Status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Remaining-work folder tree as the default Work view | Answers what remains and what is next while preserving lineage and state columns | Requires a precise remaining predicate, placement invariant, and deterministic order | Selected direction |
| Replace the table with nested cards or a node graph | Visually emphasizes hierarchy | Loses scan-friendly column alignment and does not match the requested familiar folder-tree interaction | Rejected |
| Keep an optional flat List view | Preserves global sorting and column comparison for audits | Adds a small view-mode control and another presentation path to test | Selected complement |
| One square command button per artifact | Keeps commands discoverable without widening every row | Needs accessible labeling, keyboard behavior, and compact popover placement | Selected direction |
| Descriptive menu labels with hidden exact commands | Lets operators choose by intent instead of memorizing syntax | Copy confirmation must make the selected action unambiguous | Selected direction |
| State-aware allowlisted commands | Offers continuation, review, blocker, mitigation, and release actions only when relevant | Requires one canonical mapping from artifact type/state to safe command templates | Selected direction |
| A uniform `ts: continue <artifact-id>` route | Makes every menu predictable and keeps copied text short | Would add a new canonical dispatcher that must resolve type and state deterministically | Open option |
| Existing type-specific commands only | Reuses established Tool Shed routes | Some Maps and PRMs lack one concise ID-targeted continuation command | Open option |

## Open Questions

- Which additional non-document governed cycle kinds need their own visible row in the first release,
  rather than appearing through their owning Idea, Map, PRM, or Campaign and release state?
- Which existing exact Tool Shed inspection/continuation commands form the smallest useful first
  command allowlist without adding a new generic dispatcher?

## Decisions

- Use a familiar folder-style tree with indentation and expand/collapse controls.
- Make `Work → Remaining → Tree` the default operational presentation.
- Define remaining work from unfinished lifecycle, outcome, reconciliation, closure, or release
  obligations; exclude only fully terminal and recursively closed work.
- Keep `All → List` as an explicit history/audit presentation rather than the default.
- Require one canonical tree placement per item: one owning parent or one root. Surface invalid or
  ambiguous placement under **Needs placement**.
- Preserve ancestors as subdued context when a filter or search matches only a descendant.
- Order root chains by the reported local planning projection and order siblings by explicit
  dependency/milestone/Campaign order with a deterministic stable fallback.
- Mark the first executable item as **Next** while keeping blocked, waiting, and release-pending work
  visible in its branch.
- Keep each artifact as a row with its existing operational state columns.
- Put one small square command-menu button on every displayed entry.
- Describe menu actions in plain language; the description does not need to expose the raw command.
- Clicking an action copies its exact command to the clipboard for manual paste into Codex.
- The dashboard does not execute or remotely deliver copied commands.
- Make menus specific to artifact type and current state.
- Blocked entries should offer applicable unblock or mitigation-oriented commands.
- Reuse the established local-authority and privacy boundary from prior dashboard work.

## Don't Forget

- Avoid repeating chain-level release badges on every ancestor when one tree-level rollup can explain it.
- Preserve row-specific lifecycle state so a completed Campaign awaiting Work5 is distinguishable from
  a currently working sibling.
- Keep essential menu labels and confirmation accessible to keyboard and assistive-technology users.
- Provide a clipboard fallback when the Clipboard API is unavailable.
- Prevent filtered results from becoming misleading orphan rows with no visible ancestry.
- Show matching-work and root-chain counts; do not count context-only ancestors as remaining matches.
- Keep whole root trees together across pagination.
- Validate copied commands against current canonical Tool Shed syntax; never generate arbitrary text
  from titles or descriptions.
- Use concise confirmation such as **Continue campaign command copied** without implying execution.

